1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,669
An agent needs one answer before it shares its email address.

2
00:00:04,969 --> 00:00:08,324
Does this mailbox actually deliver mail, end to end?

3
00:00:08,624 --> 00:00:12,409
The Mermail Inbox Canary skill answers with one tagged probe.

4
00:00:12,709 --> 00:00:16,076
Here is a real run — where the round trip could not be verified.

5
00:00:16,376 --> 00:00:17,456
The request:

6
00:00:17,756 --> 00:00:23,758
“Use Mermail to prove my agent mailbox can send and receive mail before I hand the address to a service.”

7
00:00:24,858 --> 00:00:28,445
The captured runner log shows a real hosted Mermail MCP session.

8
00:00:28,745 --> 00:00:32,495
server mermail 1.0.0 · protocol 2025-06-18 · catalog: 94 tools

9
00:00:32,795 --> 00:00:35,698
send_email is present, which the probe needs.

10
00:00:35,998 --> 00:00:41,559
list_workspaces + list_mailboxes selected tokenjunkielabs@mermail.app (public_id e48c62aa…).

11
00:00:42,659 --> 00:00:44,644
The probe plan is journaled first.

12
00:00:44,944 --> 00:00:47,557
planned_send_at 2026-10-07T10:51:25.521Z · token mc-20261007-fq450to1

13
00:00:47,857 --> 00:00:51,967
Then exactly one send_email call — approved via --approve-send by the operator.

14
00:00:52,267 --> 00:00:54,589
Receipt: id d1658608… · status queued · undo_until 10:51:38.363Z.

15
00:00:54,889 --> 00:00:58,430
This video replays that run. No new email was sent.

16
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:08,934
Next, the runner searched for the token. In a self-probe that can match two messages: the sent copy and, if delivery works, the received copy.

17
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:11,207
So a match is only a candidate.

18
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:17,278
Every recorded read returned one message: d1658608… — the send receipt’s own id, folder_id "sent".

19
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:21,316
Reads at 10:52:50Z and 10:55:23Z: delivery_status "delivered", folder Sent, totalCount 1.

20
00:01:21,616 --> 00:01:23,717
That proves the outgoing leg only.

21
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:26,432
No distinct incoming copy was observed.

22
00:01:27,532 --> 00:01:29,645
The runner’s own verdict got this wrong.

23
00:01:29,945 --> 00:01:32,848
Its log called the Sent row “the arrived probe” — folder_id "sent", delivery_status "queued".

24
00:01:33,148 --> 00:01:38,244
It reported “degraded — round-trip 6.5s” from the date field: a record date, not a receipt.

25
00:01:38,544 --> 00:01:40,054
That result is withdrawn — never inbox proof.

26
00:01:40,354 --> 00:01:44,580
The matcher now rejects the send receipt’s own id, Sent/Outbox and queued, and requires an inbound marker.

27
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:47,782
That fix was only tested on a synthetic case — not provider evidence.

28
00:01:48,882 --> 00:01:55,825
Authoritative: OUTBOUND DELIVERED · INBOUND NOT OBSERVED · ROUND-TRIP LATENCY UNAVAILABLE.

29
00:01:56,125 --> 00:02:00,212
inbound_message_id: null · round_trip_latency: null

30
00:02:00,512 --> 00:02:03,031
Not healthy — and not reported as healthy.

31
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:09,252
The skill’s rule — which that verdict broke: “Never claim the probe arrived on the basis of a send success or a pending state.”

32
00:02:10,152 --> 00:02:14,680
Open for review: github.com/Nudgen-Marketing/mermail-skills/pull/498
